# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b5), edited. eleanor_heyling's first conversation.

~ start
set eleanor_heyling_met
Narrator: Calfskin folios lean like chimneys around a cluttered desk. A gaunt woman with gall ink on her chin squints over wire-rimmed spectacles.
eleanor_heyling [weary]: Almanacs are third shelf on the left, mind the loose board. And do not slam the door, Sheridan just boiled the kettle.
-> menu

~ menu
* Ask about her bookshop and research -> about_work
* Ask about her family in Wickermoor -> about_village
* Browse their wares -> shop_open
* Ask what she has been reading lately -> rumour
* [Arcana DC 14] Examine the scorched marginalia on her open ledger -> check_ok | check_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ about_work
eleanor_heyling [neutral]: I sell vellum, iron-gall ink, and verified histories, see Grimshaw's Third Compendium, page forty-two. I read every volume before I part with it.
eleanor_heyling [sly]: One must know what teeth a book has before handing it to a stranger.
-> menu

~ about_village
eleanor_heyling [sad]: My late sister left me three children to raise. Margery and Algernon do most of the minding when I miss the clocktower bells.
-> menu

~ shop_open
eleanor_heyling [neutral]: Wipe your hands first, please. The parchment is dry and the ink freshly mixed.
shop
-> menu

~ rumour
eleanor_heyling [weary]: Someone took a pruning knife to the parish records before the seventy-third year, keen that we forget who planted the oak in the Round.
-> menu

~ check_ok
Narrator: The letters on her desk squirm toward her ink-stained fingertips like iron to a lodestone, leaving frost on the blotter.
eleanor_heyling [afraid]: Close it, quickly. Some chapters in Druskenvald read you back by candlelight and never let you sleep.
-> menu

~ check_fail
Narrator: She snaps a ledger shut over her notes, raising a puff of dust and dried lavender.
eleanor_heyling [neutral]: Private correspondence and tallies. My eyes are simply tired from the small print.
-> menu
